    q: 'Why does Addison’s disease darken the skin?',
    a: 'Because the same signal that tries to rescue the failing adrenal gland also tells the skin to make pigment. In primary adrenal failure the pituitary floods the body with ACTH to drive the adrenal, and ACTH’s first thirteen amino acids are identical to α-MSH, the pigment hormone. So a surge of ACTH is also a surge of melanocortin signal at MC1R in the skin, which responds by making dark eumelanin.',
  },
