    q: 'Is this the same hyperpigmentation seen with melanocortin drugs?',
    a: 'Mechanistically, yes — it is the same MC1R pigment pathway. Melanocortin agonist drugs such as setmelanotide and bremelanotide can darken skin as a side effect for exactly this reason. Addison’s disease is the natural, endogenous version: the body’s own excess melanocortin doing what the drugs do on purpose.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does treating Addison’s reverse the pigmentation?',
    a: 'Treatment is hormone replacement — cortisol (and often aldosterone) replacement managed by an endocrinologist. Restoring cortisol restores the pituitary’s brake, ACTH falls back toward normal, and the excess pigment signal eases. This page explains the biology; it is not medical advice.',
