>
  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'Addison’s disease is primary adrenal insufficiency — the adrenal glands fail to make enough cortisol — and its classic sign is a bronzing of the skin.',
      'That bronzing is pure melanocortin biology: low cortisol removes the pituitary’s brake, so it pours out POMC and ACTH to try to drive the adrenal.',
      'ACTH’s first thirteen amino acids are identical to α-MSH, the pigment signal — so a flood of ACTH is also a flood of melanocortin at MC1R in the skin.',
      'MC1R answers the way it always does: by making dark eumelanin. The darkening favours mouth, gums, scars, and skin creases — places sunlight never reaches, marking the cause as internal.',
      'It is the natural version of the skin-darkening that melanocortin agonist drugs cause on purpose — and it reverses as hormone replacement restores the feedback brake and ACTH falls.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Of all the places the melanocortin system surfaces, few are as clinically beautiful as
    this one. <strong>Addison’s disease</strong> — primary adrenal insufficiency — is a
    disorder of a missing hormone, cortisol. Yet its most famous sign is written on the skin:
    a deepening, bronze pigmentation. The connection between a failing adrenal gland and a
    darkening skin looks mysterious until you know the melanocortin system, at which point it
    becomes almost inevitable.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>

  <h2>A rescue signal overheard by the skin</h2>
  <p>
    The adrenal glands sit under the control of the pituitary, which drives them with{" "}
    <a href="/acth">ACTH</a>. Normally cortisol feeds back to the pituitary and tells it when
    to ease off — a thermostat. In Addison’s disease the adrenal cortex is damaged and
    cannot make cortisol, so that feedback signal never arrives. The pituitary, sensing no
    cortisol, does the only thing it can: it pushes harder, manufacturing ever more{" "}
    <a href="/system">POMC</a> and releasing ever more ACTH in a bid to flog a gland that
    cannot respond.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>
  <p>
    Here is the twist that makes it a pigment disease. ACTH is cut from POMC, and its first
    thirteen amino acids are <em>identical</em> to <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a>, the
    melanocortin that drives pigmentation. ACTH is, in effect, α-MSH with a tail. So when the
    pituitary floods the body with ACTH, it is also flooding it with melanocortin signal — and
    the <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> receptors on the skin’s pigment cells read that
    signal exactly as they would read a tan command, and make dark eumelanin.<Cite n={1} /> The
    rescue signal aimed at the adrenal is overheard by the skin.
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="The bronzing explained: the adrenal can't make cortisol, so the pituitary loses its brake and pours out ACTH. Because ACTH's first 13 residues are α-MSH, that surge is also a melanocortin surge at MC1R — and the skin answers with pigment. The missing cortisol and the dark skin are the same loop read at two ends."
    source="POMC/ACTH structure and the HPA axis per StatPearls NBK500031; clinical hyperpigmentation per StatPearls Addison Disease NBK441994. Schematic."
  >
    <BronzingLoop />
  </Figure>

  <h2>Where it shows, and why that matters</h2>
  <p>
    The distribution of the darkening is itself a diagnostic clue, and it is where this
    differs from a suntan. Addison’s hyperpigmentation favours sun-exposed skin but also,
    tellingly, places the sun never reaches: the inside of the cheeks and the gums, the creases
    of the palms, recent scars, and pressure points.<Cite n={2} /> A tan stops at the clothing
    line; this does not. That pattern tells a clinician the pigment is being driven from within,
    by a circulating signal, rather than from without by light — melanocortin biology leaving a
    visible signature.
  </p>

  <h2>The same pathway the drugs use</h2>
  <p>
    This is the endogenous mirror image of a theme that runs through the therapeutic pages on
    this site. Melanocortin agonist drugs — <a href="/setmelanotide">setmelanotide</a>,{" "}
    <a href="/bremelanotide">bremelanotide</a>, the <a href="/melanotan">melanotan</a> peptides —
    can all darken skin and gums as a side effect, precisely because they engage MC1R the way
    excess ACTH does.<Cite n={1} /> Addison’s disease is what happens when the body itself,
    through sheer physiological necessity, produces that same melanocortin excess. The drugs do
    deliberately, and locally, what the failing feedback loop does by accident and bodywide. For
    why skin-darkening comes built into melanocortin agonism, see{" "}
    <a href="/effects">the trade-off</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Addison’s disease is not treated through the melanocortin system — its care is cortisol
    (and usually aldosterone) replacement, and the adrenal insufficiency itself is serious and
    can be life-threatening in a crisis. But the disease earns its place here because its signature
    sign is one of the clearest readouts of melanocortin physiology in all of medicine: restore the
    cortisol, the pituitary’s brake returns, ACTH falls, and the excess pigment signal fades. For
    the hormone at the centre of it, see <a href="/acth">ACTH</a>; for the receptor that reads it in
    the skin, see <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a>; for the adrenal receptor ACTH is actually aimed
    at, see <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a>.
  </p>

